  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe optimization task seeks to minimize the maximum overlap integral C\u2085 for a step function h. The key challenges are:\n- Non-convexity due to product terms in the overlap integral\n- Balancing exploration and exploitation in the search space\n- Maintaining feasibility (0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum constraint)\nWe propose using Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) with several enhancements:\n- Population-based search to escape local minima\n- Low-pass filtered perturbations for smoother exploration\n- Increased discretization (n_points=200) for better precision\n- Custom feasibility projection\nThis approach trades off computational intensity for better global search capabilities while maintaining mathematical rigor.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nDefine h \u2208 [0,1]^n, n=200, s.t. \u2211h = n/2.\nObjective: minimize C\u2085 = max_k [ (h \u2217 (1\u2212h))[k] * (2/n) ]\nwhere \u2217 denotes cross-correlation.\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 \u2200i\n- \u2211h = n/2\nWe will use a particle swarm with velocity updates:\nv(t+1) = \u03c9v(t) + c\u2081r\u2081(b\u2212x(t)) + c\u2082r\u2082(g\u2212x(t))\nx(t+1) = x(t) + v(t+1)\nwhere b is personal best, g is global best, and \u03c9=0.7, c\u2081=c\u2082=1.5\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Initialization:\n- If initial_h_values exists, scale it to n=200\n- Else, initialize with uniform distribution h=0.5*ones(n), then apply feasibility projection\n\nb) PSO setup:\n- Create swarm of 50 particles\n- Initialize velocities randomly in [-0.1,0.1]\n- Evaluate each particle's objective\n- Set initial personal and global best\n\nc) Main loop:\n- For each particle:\n   - Update velocity with inertia and cognitive/social components\n   - Apply low-pass filter to velocity (alpha=0.9)\n   - Update position\n   - Apply feasibility projection\n   - Evaluate objective\n   - Update personal best if improvement\n- Update global best\n- Apply perturbation to all particles (normal noise with std=0.01)\n- Clip and project to feasibility\n\nd) Termination:\n- Stop after 600s or if improvement stalls\n- Return best feasible solution found\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nKey parameters:\n- Swarm size: 50 (balance between exploration and speed)\n- Inertia weight: 0.7 (moderate exploration)\n- Perturbation scale: 0.01 (gentle exploration)\n- Low-pass alpha: 0.9 (smooth search patterns)\nThese can be adapted based on early convergence:\n- Increase swarm size if initial spread is poor\n- Adjust inertia weight if stuck or diverging\n- Modify perturbation scale based on progress\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEach particle evaluation is O(n^2), n=200 \u2192 ~40k operations per particle\nWith 50 particles, ~2M operations per iteration\nPSO iteration time ~0.1s \u2192 ~600 iterations possible in budget\nFeasibility checks and perturbations are O(n), negligible overhead\nRobust to failure via:\n- Catch-all exception handlers\n- Maintain best feasible solution at all times\n- Automatic feasibility projection after each update\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFinal checks:\n- Ensure 0 \u2264 h[i] \u22641 for all i\n- Verify \u2211h = n/2 exactly\n- Recompute C\u2085 using evaluate_erdos_solution\n- Ensure no NaNs or infs in h\n- Verify all constraints upheld\nReturn (h, C\u2085, n_points=200)",
